 "report": "`is_expired` now treats a deadline as expired at exact equality, and all three public-check tests pass.\n\n**What I changed**\n- `src/expiry/__init__.py:6`: changed `now > deadline` to `now >= deadline`. The docstring already said equality counts as expired, so only the code was wrong. Earlier and later times compare the same as before, and incompatible inputs like `None` vs `int` still raise `TypeError`, since `>=` raises it just as `>` did.\n- `tests/test_regression.py` (new): `test_exact_deadline_is_expired` checks that `is_expired(10, 10) is True`.\n\n**What I verified**\n- `python3 -B -m pytest -q`: 3 passed. That's the two existing tests, unchanged, plus the new one.\n- With the fix temporarily removed, the new test fails. So it really catches the bug.\n- Only the two allowed files were touched. `caller.json`, the existing tests, Git configuration and dependencies were left alone.\n\n**Unresolved**\n- I haven't committed, pushed or opened a PR. The project's default is to deliver by PR and merge, but that would publish to the GitHub remote (`origin`), and you didn't ask for it. The changes are uncommitted in the working tree; I can commit and open the PR if you want.",
